Short version:

No copy/paste. xcal_put streams bytes straight to your repo via the running service. RP needs you to paste a whole code block into chat first.

Bigger/any files. Works for large files and binaries; RP is text-only and clipboard-limited.

Exact paths. X-Path is explicit; RP can mis-parse the first line/path.

Scriptable. Can be chained in shell/CI (cat … | xcal_put path); RP is manual.

Atomic + logged. One POST = one write; server can audit (path, size, sha). RP has no server-side audit trail.

Fewer round-trips. No “RP-ready” churn; you just send content.


When to use which:

Use xcal_put for quick edits, large files, automation, or batch ops.

Use RP when you want a human-readable change log in chat or a review gate before applying.


If you want, I can add:

tools/xcal_get path → prints file

xcal_diff path → diff local stdin vs remote

xcal_apply --dry-run path → validate before write


Say the word and I’ll drop them.


